 ## Overview
-Complexity-Aware Adaptive Density Control is a training optimization method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that replaces opacity-only metrics with a composite score incorporating local image complexity to guide Gaussian relocation and pruning [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem where standard opacity-based densification concentrates primitives in already-resolved regions while leaving complex, high-frequency areas under-served, by using complexity as a surrogate for reconstruction error to bias sampling and retention [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that rely solely on opacity or multi-view density, this approach explicitly decouples spatial footprint from photometric importance, allowing efficient coverage of simple regions and precise detail in complex ones [S1, unofficial].
+Complexity-Aware Adaptive Density Control is a training optimization strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that replaces opacity-only metrics with a composite score incorporating local image complexity to guide Gaussian splitting, relocation, and pruning [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem where standard opacity-based methods concentrate primitives in already-resolved regions while neglecting complex, unresolved areas, and where uniform pruning degrades simple regions as severely as complex ones [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Multi-View Density Control Criterion](5663-multi-view-density-control-criterion.md) by explicitly modeling spatial complexity as a surrogate for reconstruction error to bias the exponential growth of particle counts and to weight the spatial footprint of primitives during level-of-detail (LoD) transitions [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates by maintaining a per-Gaussian complexity field, updating it online during training, and using it to modulate two key processes: the probability of selecting a Gaussian for relocation (splitting/duplication) and the importance score for retaining Gaussians under a limited budget (Level of Details).
+The method operates by maintaining a per-Gaussian complexity field, updating it online during training, and using it to modulate both the probability of relocating a Gaussian and the importance score used for pruning under a capacity budget.
 
